From 15ea04bb4e0f2f5637f4f449a6edc890e4a6cff2 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Pierre Bogdanovscky <pierrebog@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 17 Sep 2026 14:00:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] Issue CSS key commands through the laser contrib

A DVD opened through the stream callbacks could not name the drive it was
reading from, so a transport that has to send an ioctl somewhere had nowhere
to send it. Android has no OS-level SCSI passthrough either, so ioctl_*()
had no branch that worked there and CSS discs in a USB optical drive could
not be decrypted.

Add dvdcss_open_stream2(), which takes that name alongside the stream
callbacks, and a branch that sends its CDBs through liblaser to the drive
the name identifies: the target is read as the registry token of a drive the
Java side opened, and the key commands are addressed to it while every
sector still arrives through the callbacks. dvdcss_open_common() already
carried both a target and a callback set, and dvdcss_check_device() and
dvdcss_open_device() both return at their stream branch before reading
psz_device, so nothing else changes.

The branch is behind enable_laser, off by default: it needs <laser.h> and
the laser_* symbols, which nowhere else has. The dependency behind the
option is required, so a build that asks for laser and cannot have it stops
at configure time rather than at the final link. dvdcss_open_stream2() is
unconditional, and on a build without the branch it records the name and
otherwise ignores it.
